<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caterina Sforza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1463">1463</a>–<a href="/years/1509">1509</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">House of Sforza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caterina Sforza was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-maria-sforza">Galeazzo Maria Sforza</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-riario">Girolamo Riario</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imola">Imola</a> and Forlì. After her husband's murder in 1488 she ruled the lordship as regent for her son. She defied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>'s siege of Forlì in 1499 to 1500 but was captured, and her state passed to the Borgias.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caterina-sforza.jpg" alt="Caterina Sforza" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italia,_caterina_riario_di_forl%C3%AC,_riproduzione_della_medaglia_del_1488_ca..JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Caterina Sforza was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1463, one of four illegitimate children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-maria-sforza">Galeazzo Maria Sforza</a> and his mistress Lucrezia Landriani. In 1473 she became betrothed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-riario">Girolamo Riario</a>, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>, who gave him the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imola">Imola</a>. She was ten, and the marriage is said to have been consummated when she was fourteen. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> she became an intermediary between the Roman court and other Italian courts, especially Milan. Her first child, Ottaviano, was born on 1 September 1479.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1480 the Pope also gave Girolamo the lordship of Forlì. Pope Innocent VIII confirmed him in both lordships, but his appointment as captain-general was only nominal. Girolamo had abolished taxes to win favour, but when the city ran out of money at the end of 1485 he was forced to levy them, which made him unpopular. After more than a half dozen failed attempts, a conspiracy led by the Orsi family, a noble family of Forlì, killed him on 14 April 1488. Caterina and her six children were made prisoners, and she then ruled the lordship as regent for her son Ottaviano.</p><p>In 1499 Louis XII came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and allied himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a>, who in return was to support his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> as ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>. A papal bull of 9 March 1499 invalidated the investiture of the feudal lords, including Caterina. Left without Florentine help, she recruited and trained soldiers, stored weapons and food and reinforced the fortress of Ravaldino, where she resided. Cesare obtained custody of her from the French general Yves d'Allègre, promising to treat her as a guest, and took her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where she was imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo">Castel Sant'Angelo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Pope accused her of trying to kill him with poisoned letters in November 1499, and historians remain unsure whether the charge was well founded. She was released on 30 June 1501 by Yves d'Allègre. She then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, where she fought a legal battle over the custody of her son Giovanni, who was returned to her in 1504. After Alexander VI's death on 18 August 1503 she pleaded her case to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>, but the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imola">Imola</a> and Forlì opposed her return, and the domain passed to Antonio Maria Ordelaffi on 22 October 1503. She died on 28 May 1509.</p><p>Caterina kept a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 454 recipes in cosmetics, chemistry and medicine, later published as Experimenti by the historian Pier Desiderio Pasolini. Her only child to inherit her militant personality was Giovanni delle Bande Nere, and she was nicknamed La Tigre. Machiavelli tells that when conspirators threatened her children she mocked them, saying that she could make more.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caterina Sforza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1473 Pope Sixtus IV acquired Imola for his nephew Girolamo Riario, who married <b>Caterina Sforza</b>, daughter of Galeazzo Maria Sforza of the Duchy of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-imola-and-forli">Lordship of Imola and Forli</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1492" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Caterina Sforza"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1492">What was here in 1492? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
